WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.049 --> 00:00:01.229
Hi!

00:00:01.229 --> 00:00:04.840
Convolutional neural networks compute convolutions
on the input like this:

00:00:04.840 --> 00:00:09.390
For an image, we take the kernel and slide
it over the input to obtain local weighted

00:00:09.390 --> 00:00:10.890
averages everywhere.

00:00:10.890 --> 00:00:11.890
Right?

00:00:11.890 --> 00:00:12.900
Not always.

00:00:12.900 --> 00:00:17.109
See, in the convolution definition the kernel
matrix appears to be flipped:

00:00:17.109 --> 00:00:21.390
As the index of the input increases, the index
of the kernel decreases.

00:00:21.390 --> 00:00:26.369
But if you do not flip the kernel, you get
the cross-correlation which is the one implemented

00:00:26.369 --> 00:00:30.179
as a convolutional layer by many of the deep
learning libraries.

00:00:30.179 --> 00:00:34.850
So, when working with a pre-trained CNN, it
is important to know whether it was trained

00:00:34.850 --> 00:00:40.910
with convolutions or with cross-correlation
to avoid unexpectedly flipped learned kernels.

00:00:40.910 --> 00:00:46.260
But we also see how convolutions are basically
just cross-correlations between input and

00:00:46.260 --> 00:00:47.679
kernel; in other words:

00:00:47.679 --> 00:00:53.329
a CNN learns a kernel that maximizes correlation
with patterns, specializing the kernel at

00:00:53.329 --> 00:00:55.350
detecting patterns in the input.

00:00:55.350 --> 00:00:59.489
It’s almost like neural networks are built
as a huge correlation detector.

